{"id":"partition-labels","title":"Partition Labels","difficulty":"Medium","pattern":"Greedy","tags":["Greedy","String","Hash Table","Two Pointers"],"companies":{"google":2,"meta":2,"amazon":3,"microsoft":1},"description":"Split the string `s` into as many contiguous parts as possible such that every letter appears in **at most one** part.\n\nReturn the lengths of the parts in order. The parts, concatenated, must give back `s`.","examples":[{"input":"s = \"ababcbacadefegdehijhklij\"","output":"[9,7,8]","explanation":"\"ababcbaca\" | \"defegde\" | \"hijhklij\". The letter a stretches from index 0 to 8, so the first part cannot end sooner."},{"input":"s = \"eccbbbbdec\"","output":"[10]","explanation":"e and c appear at both ends, so the whole string is one part."}],"constraints":["1 <= s.length <= 500","s consists of lowercase English letters."],"hints":["A part that contains a letter must extend at least to that letter's last occurrence in s.","Precompute the last index of every letter. Scan with a running `end` = max last index of letters seen in the current part.","When the scan index equals `end`, the part is complete: record its length and start a new one."],"function":"partitionLabels","signature":"def partitionLabels(self, s: str) -> List[int]:","arg_types":{"s":"str"},"return_type":"List[int]","compare":"exact","tests":[{"input":{"s":"ababcbacadefegdehijhklij"},"output":[9,7,8]},{"input":{"s":"eccbbbbdec"},"output":[10]},{"input":{"s":"a"},"output":[1]},{"input":{"s":"abcdef"},"output":[1,1,1,1,1,1]},{"input":{"s":"abab"},"output":[4]},{"input":{"s":""},"output":[200,200,200,200,200,200,200,200,200,200,200,200,200,200,200,200,200,200,200,200,200,200,200,200,200]},{"input":{"s":""},"output":[4940]}],"solution":"class Solution:\n    def partitionLabels(self, s: str) -> List[int]:\n        last = {ch: i for i, ch in enumerate(s)}\n        sizes = []\n        start = end = 0\n        for i, ch in enumerate(s):\n            end = max(end, last[ch])\n            if i == end:\n                sizes.append(end - start + 1)\n                start = i + 1\n        return sizes\n","approach":"**Each letter forces an interval `[first, last]`, and a part boundary may only be placed where no interval crosses it.** Record every letter's last index in one pass. Then scan: `end` is the farthest last-occurrence among letters seen since the current part began. The moment the scan index `i` catches up with `end`, every letter in the part has been fully used and nothing seen so far appears later, so cutting here is safe and, because we cut at the first legal position, it yields the most parts.\n\n**Why it works:** the cut positions are exactly the indices `i` where no letter occurring at or before `i` occurs after `i`. The running maximum of last occurrences is the smallest `end` satisfying that for the current part; cutting as early as possible can only increase the number of parts (merging intervals greedily from the left). This is the same mechanism as merging overlapping intervals.\n\n**Common mistakes:** recomputing the last index inside the loop with `rfind` (O(n * 26) is fine, but O(n^2) scans are not), returning the substrings instead of their lengths, and forgetting to reset `start`.\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** Return the parts rather than the sizes. Minimise the number of parts subject to a maximum part length? (Then it is no longer greedy-trivial; think DP over cut positions.) Unicode input? (Replace the 26-slot array with a dict, which the solution already does.) Relate it to Merge Intervals.","complexity":{"time":"O(n)","space":"O(1) beyond the output (at most 26 letters)"},"similar":["merge-intervals","hand-of-straights","jump-game-ii"],"number":150,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def partitionLabels(self, s: str) -> List[int]:\n        pass\n"}